    "result_text": "The full suite is green. There's only the one test class in the repo (`AcceptanceTest`), and it passes along with the build.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe membership number is derived purely from an owner's own fields, so I added it as a computed, read-only value rather than a stored column \u2014 two small, low-impact changes:\n\n1. **`MembershipNumber.java`** (new) \u2014 a tiny helper mirroring the existing `CustomerCode`/`HouseholdId` convention. It formats `<customerCode>-M<YY>`, taking `registrationDate.getYear() % 100` for the two-digit year, and returns `null` if either input is missing.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 one new `@Transient getMembershipNumber()` getter (three lines) that delegates to the helper, right beside the analogous `getHouseholdId()`.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `membershipNumber` property to the `Owner` schema. Because MapStruct maps by property name, the generated `OwnerDto` picks up the new getter automatically \u2014 no change to `OwnerMapper` or the controller was needed.\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n\nI avoided touching any large, complex class beyond a single trivial getter. The formatting logic lives in a brand-new file with zero surrounding complexity (`WMC_other = 1`), and the `Owner` change is one CC-1 method that just delegates. The controller and mapper \u2014 the higher-complexity classes \u2014 were left untouched, since the spec-driven mapping wires everything up on its own.\n\nFor `SMI-0007` registered in 2026, this yields `SMI-0007-M26`, matching the specification.",
